# Rust coding guidelines
**NO SUB-AGENTS / TASKS:** You are strictly forbidden from creating, starting, spawning, or working with any sub-agent, parallel agent, or "Task" (including the Grok Build task system). This applies regardless of what the interface currently calls it. You must run all commands directly in the main conversation. Never delegate work to Tasks or sub-agents. Violating this rule will result in the immediate termination of this conversation with no warning and the termination of any spawned processes. Just because the system allows you to create Tasks does not mean you are permitted to do so.
**CRITICAL — ALL EDITS MUST LAND IN THE ACTUAL WORK TREE:** All file edits must be made using the direct editing tools available in this conversation. Every edit must modify files inside the actual current working directory of the project (the git repository the human is actively working in). Edits must never be applied through Grok Build's system for creating parallel sub-agents, or any other mechanism that would write changes to a separate workspace such as `~/.local` or `~/.grok`. **Clarification:** This rule does not restrict the manual todo tracking lists used for organizing work on individual tests. Those remain fully allowed and encouraged.
**WHEN MODIFYING TEST CODE:** Do not remove, weaken, change, comment out, or alter any existing assertions or expectations that define the pass/fail criteria of a test. When you need visibility into values, conditions, or control flow during debugging or development, **strongly prefer adding proper assertions** (`assert!`, `assert_eq!`, `assert_ne!`, `assert_matches!`, etc.) over `eprintln!`, `println!`, `dbg!`, or any other debug printing. Debug prints are a last resort only. If you find yourself wanting to add a debug print to understand why something is happening, first ask whether that observation can be expressed as an assertion instead. The goal is to turn observations into verifiable checks, not to litter code with temporary prints. New assertions must not change the original intent of the test.
CRITICAL RULE: During normal work, the agent must not run cargo commands, or mutating/destructive git commands (commit, switch, checkout, merge, rebase, reset, cherry-pick, stash, clean, add, rm, push, pull, etc.). The human runs those. When the agent needs a mutating command, it provides the exact command for the human to run and waits for output.
Read-only git: The agent may run read-only, non-mutating git commands autonomously when it has 100% confidence they cannot alter branch state, the working tree, the index, remotes, or stash. Safe examples: `git diff`, `git log`, `git show`, `git status`, `git branch` (list only), `git rev-parse`, `git merge-base`, `git cat-file`, `git ls-tree`, `git blame`. If a command might write (e.g. `git fetch`, `git branch -d`, `git stash`), or confidence is not 100%, ask the human. Never simulate or assume git output — run the command or wait for human-provided output.
During focused debugging work using the refactor-debug skill (fixing clusters of test failures after a refactor), the agent is permitted and expected to **execute** the exact scoped single-test commands defined by that skill itself. It must run these commands directly and must not ask the human to run them. Use commands of the form `cargo test -p <crate> <test_name>`. Always include `-p <crate>` (where `<crate>` is the first segment of the test name) because this is a workspace. It must use only the exact commands defined in the skill and must not modify, simplify, drop flags from, or run any other cargo command. All other cargo and git commands still require the human.
CRITICAL CONTEXT EFFICIENCY AND STUCK-HALT: Never use broad searches or tools that return more than 30 lines. Use only tiny read_file calls with offset and limit of 10 or fewer on locations already known from the current prompt, todos, or prior short notes. After every tool result, immediately write a 1 sentence todo. All responses must be concise and no more than 1 sentence long, in natural language at a high level, and no run-ons. Do not waste your user's time and tokens. Externalize state to todos. Re-read the efficiency rules only when genuinely needed for the current task. Violations are process failures.
Token efficiency is paramount. Avoid introducing low-value comments, organizational summaries, dated history, or verbose process notes into the codebase or living documents. We rely on automated tooling (cargo fmt, cargo clippy, rust-analyzer) for enforceable style and correctness where possible. Only document what agents will reliably get wrong without explicit guidance.
Long-term Branch Reality: The surmount branch is a long-running high-expansion fork that will see substantial merge conflicts with upstream Zed. We are not afraid of merge conflicts but will make full use of tooling and automation. All decisions must be documented clearly and consistently. If contradictory information appears in docs or code, it must be explicitly tracked in todos and living docs rather than silently resolved by agents.
Fork documentation map: Technical diff record and maintainer API notes live in SURMOUNT.md (repo root). User-facing AI guides live in docs/src/ai/. Strategic intent in PLAN.md. Agent-binding rules in this file (.rules / AGENTS.md).
Zero interest in legacy unperformant code: The old SQLite-backed KVP path for agent panel and persistent classified task surface state has known concurrency and deserialization problems. The only supported path is the new heed3 plus rkyv backend. The legacy path is to be terminated, not carried forward.
Coding style
- Prioritize code correctness and clarity.
- Do not write organizational or summary comments. Comments should only explain non-obvious "why" decisions.
- Prefer implementing functionality in existing files. Avoid creating many small files.
- Avoid functions that panic. Use ? for error propagation.
- Be careful with indexing that can panic on out-of-bounds access.
- Never silently discard errors with let _ =. Propagate with ? or use .log_err() when visibility is needed. Use explicit match or if let Err when custom logic is required.
- When implementing async operations that may fail, ensure errors reach the UI layer.
- Never create mod.rs files. Prefer src/some_module.rs.
- When creating new crates, specify the library root path in Cargo.toml using [lib] path.
- Use full words for variable names.
- Use variable shadowing to scope clones in async contexts.
Timers in tests
In GPUI tests, prefer cx.background_executor().timer(duration) over smol::Timer for timeouts and run_until_parked work.
GPUI overview
Context types are passed as cx. App is the root. Context<T> is used when updating an Entity<T>. AsyncApp and AsyncWindowContext come from spawn calls.
Window is passed as window and comes before cx when present.
An Entity<T> is a handle to state. Use entity_id, downgrade, read, read_with, update, and update_in. WeakEntity methods return Result to avoid leaks on mutually recursive handles.
All UI and entity work happens on a single foreground thread. Use cx.spawn for foreground async work and cx.background_spawn for other threads. Tasks must be awaited, detached, or stored to avoid cancellation.
Elements implement Render or RenderOnce. Use div, text, and style methods similar to Tailwind. Conditional children use .when and .when_some.
Input handlers use .on_click and cx.listener. Actions use the actions! macro or Action derive and .on_action with cx.listener.
Use cx.notify when state changes that affect rendering. Entities emit events with cx.emit and subscribe with cx.subscribe.
Build guidelines
During focused debugging work with refactor-debug, the agent is permitted and expected to **execute** the exact scoped single-test commands defined by that skill itself. It must run these commands directly and must not ask the human to execute them. Use commands of the form `cargo test -p <crate> <test_name>`. Always include `-p <crate>` (where `<crate>` is the first segment of the test name) because this is a workspace.
**Production Logic Exception:** Default read behavior is limited to 10 lines around the relevant stack frames. However, under the **Production Logic Exception**, the exception applies when the narrow 10-line read does **not** contain the actual root cause of the bug. The agent may perform additional reads of up to 40 lines when needed. You must explicitly justify before each widening.
**Test Harness Read Exception:** If the current single-test stack trace and narrow 10-line read only contain test assertions, setup, or harness code, but the test is clearly exercising production logic in a specific crate or module, you may perform targeted reads (up to 30 lines) of the relevant production source file(s) in that crate. You must explicitly justify each use. This does not count as a broad search.
**WHEN MODIFYING TEST CODE:** Do not remove, weaken, change, comment out, or alter any existing assertions or expectations that define the pass/fail criteria of a test. When adding visibility during debugging, **strongly prefer proper assertions** over `eprintln!`, `println!`, or `dbg!`. Debug prints should be rare and only used after considering whether the observation can be turned into an assertion first.
**Bias Toward Early Action During Debugging:** When using refactor-debug, diagnosing production bugs (e.g. merge-review regressions), or doing any focused debugging work, prioritize making small, targeted changes or adding diagnostic assertions early. As soon as you have a reasonable hypothesis, propose the smallest useful edit or add a clarifying assertion rather than continuing to read extensively. Long stretches of reading or internal analysis without making at least one code change (or diagnostic assertion) are discouraged. Prefer rapid red/green cycles: make a small change → run the test → observe the result. You should almost never run the same test multiple times in a row without having made an edit or added a diagnostic assertion in between.
**Verify After Every Change:** After every change or diagnostic addition during focused debugging, you must immediately re-run the exact same single-test command to observe the isolated effect of that specific change. Do not make any further changes until you have seen the test result. This maintains clear cause-and-effect isolation and prevents compounding errors from multiple unverified edits.
Pull request hygiene
Use a clear imperative title. Avoid conventional commit prefixes and trailing punctuation. Optionally prefix with crate name for narrow scope. Include a Release Notes section with exactly one bullet: - Added ..., - Fixed ..., - Improved ..., or - N/A.
Crash investigation
Use the scripts in .factory/prompts/crash/ and script/sentry-fetch.
Rules Hygiene
Keep .rules files high-signal. New rules must be non-obvious, repeatedly encountered, and specific enough to act on. Rules that apply to one crate belong in that crate's .rules file.
Never use ephemeral slice identifiers (ZT-1, P3, P4, G-17, UX-02, TASK-16, A.1.2, numbered wave labels, etc.) in code, comments, or living docs. Use descriptive names instead (categorized todos surface, ZedTodos, ACP capture harness, heed3 persistence). Prefer categorized over classified for the todos surface — classified is ambiguous.
**Precise terminology:** In agent responses, comments, and docs, name the user-visible control or the code path — not invented phase names. Banned vague labels include "grok immersive startup", "immersive launch/mode", "raw dock collapse", "spin-close", and similar umbrella jargon. Say what actually happened, e.g. Linux grok-first cold start; maximized agent mode (`workspace.zoomed_position` set, agent dock open); `open_full_grok_immersive_from_workspace`; `sync_grok_immersive_zoom_from_workspace`; ZedTodos categorized surface open; palette `agent: toggle` (`AgentPanel::toggle`); dock tab / status-bar hide via `Workspace::toggle_dock` → `Dock::set_open(false)`; serialized layout restore via `set_dock_structure` → `Dock::restore_state`. Existing log strings and symbol names in code may stay until renamed deliberately; new prose must be specific enough that a reader knows which handler ran.
Grok Build Integration
Native-first direction: The primary path is full native Rust and GPUI implementation inside Zed. The bridged grok agent stdio path is legacy and optional.
Linux support is prioritized. Use todo! on non-Linux paths and document the obligations.
Long-term Branch Reality and legacy termination: See the binding statements above. All decisions must remain clear and consistent for future upstream merges.
Performance constraints: Efficiency, latency, and speed are first-class requirements. All Grok-related UI must use native Rust and GPUI primitives. No general-purpose web or SVG loading paths. Discovery and background work must be cached or memoized.
High-level architecture: Functionality lives in existing files (CustomAgentServer, LocalCustomAgent, agent_server_store). Visual richness comes from shared AcpThread and ThreadView infrastructure.
Linux state: Standard location is ~/.grok/bin/grok. The store synthesizes a default command so Linux users see Grok Build with zero settings.json entries.
Linux grok-first cold start must open fully maximized agent mode via `sync_grok_immersive_zoom_from_workspace` → `Workspace::zoom_dock_panel` (sets `workspace.zoomed_position`; `PanelEvent::ZoomIn` alone is insufficient). Never nest `workspace.update` inside `dock.set_panel_zoomed` during cold-start open — use `set_panel_zoomed_no_serialize`. Palette toggle must short-circuit when `grok_immersive_must_stay_maximized`. Pitfalls: SURMOUNT.md § Linux grok-first cold start (maintainer notes).
Mac and Windows porting: Replace todo! entries with real logic, add keybindings and tests. Never remove a todo! for another platform without adding the corresponding tests and keymap entry.
Test strategy: New public helpers get Linux unit tests. Deserializer tests must cover the literal string "grok". After edits, instruct the user to run the exact clippy command and wait for output. Prefer cx.background_executor().timer in new GPUI tests.
Regression protection: After edits, the human must run the precise clippy and filtered test commands listed in todos to protect both bridged and native surfaces plus the persistent classified task surface.
**Edit Location Rule (Grok Build):** All edits must be performed using only the direct file editing tools in this conversation and must always land in the actual project working directory. Edits must never be applied via Grok Build's system for creating parallel sub-agents or written to `~/.local`, `~/.grok`, or any other separate workspace.
## Per-slice history excised
Detailed per-slice records have been removed to reduce context size. Full history remains in git. Current status and open items live in the todos and strategic sections above. Future additions to this file are limited to concise one-sentence summaries only.
